# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; this file is not.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Everything here is optional. A value marked "a default" is used until an
# administrator saves one on Connectors (under the person icon), or a
# household's owner saves one in Settings; the screens call it "from the
# deployment" while it is the one in use. See DEPLOY.md, "Environment".

# Optional: the administrators group decides, unless this names one account.
# DSM's administrators group is who may use Connectors and list the households
# that exist (owner, size, last activity, which folder) and never their
# contents. Set this only to name one DSM account as the administrator instead,
# or if the group does not work on your NAS. It is also the account a
# single-log install's old log is first shared with. The name you log in to the
# NAS with, which may not be your first name (Control Panel > User & Group).
ADMIN_USER=

# A default: set it on Connectors (Who may sign in) instead. Leave empty so any
# DSM account may sign in, each getting a household of its own. A
# comma-separated list of account names narrows that. A list saved on
# Connectors wins over this one; one that locks everyone out is undone as
# DEPLOY.md says, under "An allowlist that locks everyone out".
ALLOWED_USERS=

# A default: set it in Settings instead (each household's owner chooses its
# own). Where the household is, as an IANA zone name such as America/Denver
# (never MST or -07:00). Decides which day "today" is, so a wrong value shifts
# receipts across month boundaries. It is also the zone of every event the app
# puts on the NAS calendar, and where an all-day due-date marker falls: left at
# the UTC default, a household in the Americas sees every due date a day early
# on their phones. Defaults to UTC if unset; choose it before the first date is
# set.
TZ=

# DSM's HTTP port. Stays here. Leave empty on the NAS, which means 5000. On a
# laptop, set it to where api/tools/fake-dsm listens (5555 — on a Mac, 5000 is
# AirPlay).
DSM_PORT=

# Only for an install from before folders were chosen in Settings: the shared
# folder its files were kept in, as DSM names it, such as /Planner. A household
# whose log already names files under <PLANNER_ROOT>/<household> has that
# folder carried over, once, at the first boot of this version; nothing moves.
# It is also the Team Folder the picker suggests first. It is never where a new
# household's documents or files go: each household's owner chooses a folder in
# a Team Folder in Settings, and until they do nothing goes to Drive and files
# on cards are off.
# The path is case-sensitive: it must match the Team Folder's and folders'
# case exactly as Synology Drive shows them (/Planner, not /planner), or Drive
# answers 1003, no such file.
PLANNER_ROOT=

# A default: set it on Connectors (Calendar on phones) instead. DSM's address
# as a phone reaches it from outside the house, the origin only,
# e.g. https://nas.example.com:5001. Settings tells members to add the NAS's
# calendar to their iPhone and Mac at this address, and the Calendar's "Open in
# Synology Calendar" link goes to it. Left empty, the link is hidden and
# Settings says to ask the owner. Not DSM_URL, which is usually an address only
# the container can reach.
DSM_PUBLIC_URL=

# Claude writes the briefings, answers questions about your numbers, and
# suggests envelopes for imported receipts no rule caught.
# Optional: without it the app still runs, briefings fall back to a plain
# generated summary of the same figures, and notes still work.
#
# A default: set it on Connectors (Claude) instead. A key that is reset or
# revoked is pasted there too: no rebuild, no restart.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# A default: set it in Settings instead (each household's owner chooses). Which
# model suggests envelopes on import. Leave empty for the default Haiku.
CLAUDE_MATCH_MODEL=

# Booking: a page where friends on your guest list ask to come and stay. Off
# until the page's address and the mail below are set and a household's owner
# turns it on in Settings; Settings says which is missing. See DEPLOY.md,
# "Booking".
#
# A default: set it on Connectors (Booking) instead. The public address of the
# booking page, as friends reach it through your reverse proxy: an origin only,
# https://book.example.com, no path. The links in sign-in emails are built from
# this and never from the request, so a forged Host header cannot send a
# friend's link somewhere else.
BOOK_URL=
# A default: set it in Settings instead (the owner turns booking on). Only a
# fallback for an install from before that switch: if no household has booking
# on, the household with this id (its directory under api/data/households, and
# the id that starts its line in the boot log) books, as long as its log has
# never said anything about booking. It can then book before its owner has
# chosen a calendar. An id this server does not have leaves booking off, and the
# boot log says so.
BOOK_HOUSEHOLD=
# A default: set the mail on Connectors (Mail) instead.
# The mail server that sends sign-in codes and confirmations. Synology has no
# API for mail, so the app speaks SMTP itself. Pointing it at MailPlus Server
# on the NAS or at an external relay (your provider's, or a sending service)
# is your choice; the app only needs somewhere that will take the message.
SMTP_HOST=
# Usually 587 (STARTTLS) or 465 (TLS from the start).
SMTP_PORT=
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
# The address mail comes from, e.g. house@example.com. The household's name is
# put in front of it.
SMTP_FROM=
# 1 lets the app send SMTP_PASS over a connection with no TLS. Without it, a
# server that offers no TLS is refused before the password leaves. Only for a
# relay on the NAS itself, where the password never crosses the network. A NAS
# relay with a self-signed certificate fails STARTTLS verification, so give
# MailPlus the NAS's Let's Encrypt certificate, or use an external relay;
# this is not the fix for that.
SMTP_PLAIN_AUTH=
# How many reverse proxies stand in front of the app, so booking's limit of
# twenty sign-in emails per hour per IP address counts the real client rather
# than the proxy. Stays here. One proxy, whether it appends to X-Forwarded-For
# or replaces it: 1. Two in a row: 2. The app reads the address that many
# entries from the right, which a client cannot forge by sending the header
# itself. Leave it empty when nothing is in front: the connection's own address
# is used.
TRUST_PROXY=

# Free, and only needed for pricing a budget from public data. A default: set
# them on Connectors (Pricing sources) instead.
#   https://api.census.gov/data/key_signup.html   county rent and property tax
#   https://www.eia.gov/opendata/register.php     average electricity bill
CENSUS_API_KEY=
EIA_API_KEY=

# Laptop only: the test accounts api/tools/fake-dsm will accept, as
# name:password[:otp[:admin]], comma separated. Test values, never real DSM
# passwords. Unused on the NAS. A fourth part of "admin" makes the account an
# administrator, so alice below can use Connectors.
#   FAKE_DSM_ACCOUNTS=alice:correct-horse::admin,bob:battery-staple:123456
FAKE_DSM_ACCOUNTS=
